window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-morphology","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Morphology of Flowering Plants · Practice","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the androecium is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Each stamen consists of a filament and an anther, and pollen grains are produced in the pollen sacs","In the family Solanaceae, the five stamens are epipetalous","Polyandrous condition refers to stamens united into many bundles","Epipetalous stamens show union of stamens with members of another whorl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Polyandrous means the stamens remain free. Union into more than two bundles is called polyadelphous, as in citrus. The other statements agree with NCERT, including that epipetalous stamens are fused to the petals of the corolla.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the floral characters of Solanaceae is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Calyx has five united, persistent sepals with valvate aestivation","Stamens are five and epipetalous","Ovary is inferior and unilocular with parietal placentation","Flowers are bisexual and actinomorphic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The Solanaceae ovary is superior and bilocular, with a swollen axile placenta bearing many ovules. Parietal placentation in a one-chambered ovary is characteristic of mustard, not Solanaceae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A stamen in a flower has a normal filament but does not produce any pollen grains. What is such a sterile stamen called?","image":"","options":["Staminode","Pistillode","Carpel","Perianth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT calls a sterile stamen a staminode. A pistillode is a sterile carpel, not a stamen, and carpels and perianth are not parts of the androecium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about seeds:\n(a) The hilum is a scar marking the point where the developing seed was attached to the fruit.\n(b) The micropyle is a small pore located just below the hilum.\n(c) In maize, the outer covering of the endosperm is a proteinous aleurone layer.\n(d) The coleoptile is the sheath that encloses the radicle.\n(e) In gram, the endosperm is absent in the mature seed, so reserve food is stored in the fleshy cotyledons.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(a), (c) and (e) only","(a), (b) and (e) only","(c), (d) and (e) only","(a), (c) and (d) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (e) agree with NCERT. Statement (b) is wrong because the micropyle lies above the hilum, not below it. Statement (d) is wrong because the coleoptile encloses the plumule, while the coleorhiza encloses the radicle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of a carpel is usually present at the tip of the style and serves as the receptive surface for pollen grains?","image":"","options":["Ovary","Placenta","Ovule","Stigma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The stigma sits at the tip of the style and receives pollen grains. The ovary is the enlarged basal part, and it bears ovules on the placenta.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower is described as bracteate when it has:","image":"","options":["Sepals fused into a tube","A reduced leaf at the base of its pedicel","Floral parts in multiples of three","A thalamus that encloses the ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A bract is a reduced leaf at the base of the pedicel, and flowers that bear one are called bracteate. Flowers without bracts are called ebracteate.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: An anther is usually bilobed, and each lobe has two chambers called pollen-sacs.\nStatement II: In a stamen, pollen grains are produced inside the filament.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A typical anther has two lobes, each with two pollen-sacs, so it has four pollen-sacs in all. Pollen grains are produced in these pollen-sacs, not in the filament. The filament is only the stalk that bears the anther.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Condition of stamens)\nA. Monoadelphous\nB. Diadelphous\nC. Polyadelphous\nD. Epiphyllous\nList-II (Example)\nI. Pea\nII. Lily\nIII. China rose\nIV. Citrus\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"China rose has stamens united into one bundle (monoadelphous), and pea has two bundles (diadelphous). Citrus has more than two bundles (polyadelphous), and lily has stamens attached to the perianth (epiphyllous). The usual trap is swapping pea and china rose.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the wheat plant, the root system consists of a large number of roots arising from the base of the stem.\nReason (R): In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by many roots.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Wheat is a monocot whose primary root dies early. It is replaced by numerous roots from the stem base, forming a fibrous system, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The androecium of mustard is written as $A_{2+4}$ in its floral formula, and NCERT cites mustard as an example of variation in filament length. Which interpretation is correct?","image":"","options":["Six stamens united into two bundles, i.e., diadelphous","Six free stamens in two groups, with filaments not all of the same length","Six stamens that are all attached to the petals","Two fertile stamens and four staminodes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The '+' in a floral formula separates groups of parts. Fusion would be shown by brackets and adhesion by a line above the symbols, so mustard has six free stamens in two groups with unequal filaments. Reading '2+4' as two bundles is the key trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A banana plant produces fruits even though its ovaries were never fertilised. What is such a fruit called?","image":"","options":["Drupe","Parthenocarpic fruit","Endospermic fruit","Apocarpous fruit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A fruit formed without fertilisation of the ovary is called a parthenocarpic fruit. A drupe is a type of fruit defined by the structure of its pericarp, not by whether fertilisation took place.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which region of the root tip are root hairs formed?","image":"","options":["Region of meristematic activity","Root cap","Region of elongation","Region of maturation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Cells from the elongation zone differentiate in the region of maturation. Here some epidermal cells grow into fine, thread-like root hairs. They do not form in the dividing or elongating zones.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the floral formula of a flowering plant, what does the symbol P represent?","image":"","options":["Pedicel","Perianth","Petals","Placenta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"P stands for perianth. It is used when calyx and corolla are not distinct, as in lily. Petals are shown by C (corolla), so 'Petals' is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower whose sepals are united with one another is described as having a calyx that is","image":"","options":["Polysepalous","Gamosepalous","Gamopetalous","Polypetalous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Gamosepalous means the sepals are united, and polysepalous means they are free. The terms gamopetalous and polypetalous describe petals, not sepals.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A flower is regarded as a modified shoot.\nReason (R): In a flower, the shoot apical meristem changes into a floral meristem, the internodes do not elongate and the axis becomes condensed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The flower is treated as a modified shoot precisely because its apical meristem becomes a floral meristem. Its axis is condensed with unelongated internodes, and floral appendages form at successive nodes in place of leaves. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of placentation) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\nA. Marginal\nB. Axile\nC. Parietal\nD. Basal\nList-II:\nI. Ovules on the inner wall; a false septum makes the ovary two-chambered\nII. A single ovule attached to a placenta at the base of the ovary\nIII. Ovules on a ridge along the ventral suture in two rows\nIV. Ovules attached to an axial placenta in a multilocular ovary","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Marginal placentation has a ridge along the ventral suture, and axile placentation has an axial placenta in a multilocular ovary. Parietal placentation bears ovules on the wall and has a false septum, while basal placentation has one ovule at the base.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I (Condition of stamens)\nA. Monoadelphous\nB. Diadelphous\nC. Polyadelphous\nD. Epipetalous\nList-II (Example)\nI. Citrus\nII. Brinjal\nIII. China rose\nIV. Pea\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"China rose is monoadelphous, pea is diadelphous, citrus is polyadelphous and brinjal is epipetalous. A common slip is to swap china rose and pea.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The term aestivation refers to","image":"","options":["the arrangement of ovules within the ovary","the mode of arrangement of sepals or petals in the floral bud with respect to other members of the same whorl","the arrangement of flowers on the floral axis","the pattern of arrangement of leaves on the stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aestivation describes how sepals or petals sit relative to their neighbours in the same whorl of the bud. The other options define placentation, inflorescence and phyllotaxy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student describes a fleshy fruit and its seed with the following statements:\n(i) The fruit is a ripened ovary, so its wall (pericarp) comes from the ovary wall.\n(ii) The hard stone around the seed of mango is the endocarp.\n(iii) The coir (fibre) of coconut is the endocarp.\n(iv) The seed inside develops from the fertilised ovule.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii) and (iii) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The fruit and its pericarp come from the ovary and its wall, the seed comes from the ovule, and the stony mango layer is the endocarp. Statement (iii) is wrong because coconut fibre is the fibrous mesocarp, not the endocarp.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"What is the hilum of a dicotyledonous seed?","image":"","options":["A small pore through which water enters the seed","The inner layer of the seed coat","A scar on the seed coat where the developing seed was attached to the fruit","The point where the radicle joins the cotyledons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The hilum is the scar left on the seed coat where the developing seed was attached to the fruit. The small pore just above it is the micropyle, and the inner layer of the seed coat is the tegmen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Endosperm, a product of double fertilization in angiosperms is absent in the seeds of","image":"","options":["Coconut","Orchids","Maize","Castor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Coconut, maize and castor all have endospermic (albuminous) seeds. Orchid seeds are tiny and lack endosperm at maturity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of placentation) with List-II (example).\nList-I:\nA. Marginal\nB. Axile\nC. Parietal\nD. Free central\nList-II:\nI. Dianthus\nII. Argemone\nIII. Pea\nIV. Lemon","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The correct matches are:\n- Marginal: pea.\n- Axile: lemon, which has a multilocular ovary with an axial placenta.\n- Parietal: Argemone, where a false septum develops.\n- Free central: Dianthus, which has a central axis without septa.\nThe common trap is interchanging the parietal and free central examples.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A fruit that develops from the ovary without fertilisation is called a:","image":"","options":["Drupe","Parthenocarpic fruit","Berry","Capsule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A fruit is normally a ripened ovary formed after fertilisation. When the ovary develops into a fruit without fertilisation, the fruit is called parthenocarpic. Drupe, berry and capsule are fruit types that normally form after fertilisation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a longitudinal view of a root tip, a zone lies a few millimetres above the root cap. Its cells are very small, thin-walled and have dense protoplasm, and they divide repeatedly. This zone is the:","image":"","options":["Region of elongation","Region of maturation","Region of meristematic activity","Root hair zone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Small, thin-walled, densely cytoplasmic cells that divide repeatedly mark the region of meristematic activity, which lies just above the root cap. The region of elongation lies proximal to it, and that is where cells enlarge rather than divide. Root hairs arise further back, in the region of maturation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the floral bud of china rose, one margin of each petal overlaps the next petal, and this continues around the whorl. This type of aestivation is","image":"","options":["Valvate","Imbricate","Twisted","Vexillary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When each petal overlaps the next one in a regular sequence, the aestivation is twisted. China rose, lady's finger and cotton show this type.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived.\nStatement II: In a tap root system, the secondary and tertiary roots arise from the base of the stem.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Monocots do have a short-lived primary root. In a tap root system, however, secondary and tertiary roots are laterals borne on the primary root, not on the stem base. Roots arising from the stem base are characteristic of the fibrous system.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: In the banyan tree and Monstera, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle and are called adventitious roots.\nStatement II: In mustard, the primary root formed by direct elongation of the radicle bears lateral roots of several orders, and together they constitute the tap root system.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both statements match NCERT. Adventitious roots arise from any part other than the radicle, as in grass, Monstera and banyan. Mustard has a tap root system made of the primary root with its secondary, tertiary and further laterals.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of aestivation) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Valvate\nB. Twisted\nC. Imbricate\nD. Vexillary\nList-II\nI. Gulmohur\nII. Bean\nIII. Calotropis\nIV. Lady's finger\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The matches are Calotropis (valvate: margins only touch), lady's finger (twisted), gulmohur (imbricate: overlap in no particular direction) and bean (vexillary). A common trap is placing gulmohur, a zygomorphic flower, under vexillary.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of a carpel acts as the receptive surface for pollen grains?","image":"","options":["Ovary","Style","Stigma","Placenta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The stigma, usually at the tip of the style, receives pollen grains. The style only connects the stigma to the ovary, and the placenta bears the ovules.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In wheat plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by a large number of roots. From which part of the plant do these replacing roots arise?","image":"","options":["Base of the stem","Tip of the radicle","Secondary roots of the primary root","Axils of the leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In monocots like wheat, the primary root soon dies. Many roots then arise from the base of the stem and form the fibrous root system. Roots that develop from the radicle and its laterals make up a tap root system instead.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding root systems is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In mustard, the primary root and its branches together constitute the tap root system","In wheat, the roots of the fibrous system originate from the base of the stem","In Monstera, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle","Synthesis of plant growth regulators is carried out by the shoot system only and not by roots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists synthesis of plant growth regulators as one of the main functions of the root system, so the claim that only the shoot does this is wrong. The other three statements agree with the text.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In epigynous flowers, the margin of the thalamus grows upward, encloses the ovary and fuses with it, so the other floral parts arise above the ovary","A flower bearing only carpels is unisexual","Plum and guava both have a half-inferior ovary because their floral parts arise on the rim of the thalamus","When a shoot tip transforms into a flower, that flower is always solitary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plum is perigynous with a half-inferior ovary, but guava is epigynous with an inferior ovary. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A typical anther is bilobed and each lobe has two chambers. How many pollen sacs are present in such an anther?","image":"","options":["Two","Four","One","Eight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"There are two lobes and each lobe has two chambers, so the anther has 2 × 2 = 4 pollen sacs. These sacs are where pollen grains are produced.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the embryo of a maize grain, the radicle is enclosed in a protective sheath. What is this sheath called?","image":"","options":["Coleoptile","Scutellum","Coleorhiza","Aleurone layer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In maize, the coleorhiza sheathes the radicle and the coleoptile sheathes the plumule. Students often swap these two terms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"What is the arrangement of ovules within the ovary called?","image":"","options":["Aestivation","Placentation","Phyllotaxy","Venation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Placentation is the arrangement of ovules inside the ovary. Aestivation refers to the arrangement of sepals or petals in the bud, and phyllotaxy to the arrangement of leaves on the stem.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The region of the stem at which leaves are borne is called the:","image":"","options":["Internode","Node","Axil","Rachis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Leaves arise at the nodes. Internodes are the portions of the stem between two successive nodes. The axil is the angle between a leaf and the stem, and the rachis is the axis of a pinnately compound leaf.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the family Solanaceae:\n(a) The calyx is persistent and shows valvate aestivation.\n(b) The five stamens are epiphyllous.\n(c) The ovary is superior and bilocular, with axile placentation.\n(d) Leaves are alternate, usually exstipulate, with parallel venation.\n(e) The fruit is a berry or capsule, and the seeds are many and endospermous.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(a), (b) and (c) only","(a), (c) and (e) only","(b), (d) and (e) only","(a), (c), (d) and (e) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In Solanaceae the stamens are epipetalous, not epiphyllous, so (b) is wrong. The venation is reticulate, not parallel, so (d) is wrong. Statements (a), (c) and (e) match the NCERT description of the family.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which structure covers the apex of a root like a thimble and protects the tender tip as it moves through the soil?","image":"","options":["Root hair","Root cap","Region of maturation","Coleorhiza"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The root cap is a thimble-like covering at the root apex that shields the delicate tip as it pushes through the soil. Coleorhiza is a sheath around the radicle in monocot seeds, not a part of the growing root tip.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a pinnately compound leaf such as that of neem, the leaflets are borne on a common axis called the rachis. What does the rachis represent?","image":"","options":["The petiole of the leaf","The midrib of the leaf","The leaf base","A modified axillary branch"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The rachis is the common axis that bears the leaflets, and it represents the midrib of the leaf. The incisions of the lamina reach the midrib and break the blade into leaflets. It is not the petiole. In palmately compound leaves the leaflets arise from the tip of the petiole instead.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student finds a green, flattened structure with a midrib and veins attached to a plant axis. Which observation would best confirm that it is a complete leaf and not a leaflet of a compound leaf?","image":"","options":["It has a prominent midrib with veins","It contains chlorophyll and photosynthesises","Its veinlets form a network","A bud is present in its axil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A leaf develops at a node and bears a bud in its axil, whereas the leaflets of a compound leaf do not have axillary buds. A midrib, chlorophyll and reticulate veinlets are found in leaflets as well, so they cannot tell the two apart.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ovary of a hypogynous flower is described as superior.\nReason (R): In a hypogynous flower, the gynoecium occupies the highest position on the thalamus and the other floral parts are situated below it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The ovary is called superior precisely because the gynoecium sits above the other whorls, as in mustard, china rose and brinjal. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A plant has leaves whose bases form a sheath around the stem and whose veins run parallel to each other within the lamina. Which other feature is this plant most likely to show?","image":"","options":["A persistent tap root developing from the radicle","A fibrous root system arising from the base of the stem","Reticulate venation in its younger leaves","Two cotyledons in its seed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A sheathing leaf base and parallel venation both point to a monocot. Monocots typically have a short-lived primary root that is replaced by fibrous roots arising from the stem base. A tap root, reticulate venation and two cotyledons are dicot features.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the family Solanaceae are correct?\n(i) Leaves are exstipulate with reticulate venation\n(ii) Calyx is persistent with valvate aestivation\n(iii) Stamens are five and epipetalous\n(iv) Ovary is inferior and unilocular with parietal placentation\n(v) Gynoecium is bicarpellary, obliquely placed and syncarpous\n(vi) Seeds are few and non-endospermous","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. Solanaceae has a superior, bilocular ovary with axile placentation on a swollen placenta, which rules out (iv). Its seeds are many and endospermous, which rules out (vi).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The lamina of a leaf is deeply incised, but none of the incisions reaches the midrib. This leaf should be classified as:","image":"","options":["Pinnately compound, because the lamina is divided","Palmately compound, because the lobes radiate from one point","Simple, because the incisions do not touch the midrib","Compound, because the lamina is not entire"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A leaf is simple when its lamina is entire, or when its incisions do not reach the midrib. A leaf becomes compound only when the incisions reach the midrib and break the lamina into separate leaflets; the depth of the lobes alone does not make it compound.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of phyllotaxy) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Alternate\nB. Opposite\nC. Whorled\nList-II\n(i) Alstonia\n(ii) Guava\n(iii) Mustard\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mustard has one leaf per node (alternate) and guava has a pair of leaves per node (opposite). Alstonia has more than two leaves per node, forming a whorl.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The corolla of pea shows vexillary aestivation.\nReason (R): In vexillary aestivation, the keel petals overlap the wings, which in turn overlap the standard.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pea does show vexillary aestivation, so A is true. R is false because it reverses the overlap: the standard overlaps the wings, and the wings overlap the keel.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The mature seeds of plants such as gram and peas, possess no endosperm, because","image":"","options":["These plants are not angiosperms","There is no double fertilization in them","Endosperm is not formed in them","Endosperm gets used up by the developing embryo during seed development"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Gram and pea are angiosperms, and endosperm does form after double fertilization. However, the developing embryo consumes it completely, so the mature seeds are non-endospermic and store food in the cotyledons.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A flower is regarded as a modified shoot.\nReason (R): In a flower, the internodes elongate greatly so that the floral whorls are widely separated on the axis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A flower is a modified shoot in which the shoot apical meristem becomes a floral meristem. In a flower the internodes do not elongate and the axis becomes condensed, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The stem of many flowering plants is green when young.\nReason (R): The stem of many flowering plants later becomes woody and dark brown.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true: young stems are generally green and later often become woody and dark brown. However, R describes a later change in the stem and does not explain why young stems are green.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following plants are the stamens united into two bundles (diadelphous condition)?","image":"","options":["China rose","Citrus","Brinjal","Pea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Pea has diadelphous stamens. China rose has stamens in one bundle (monoadelphous), citrus has many bundles (polyadelphous), and brinjal has epipetalous stamens.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a racemose inflorescence, the main axis continues to grow. In what order are the flowers borne on it?","image":"","options":["Basipetal succession, with the oldest flower at the top","Acropetal succession, with the oldest flowers at the base","A single terminal flower only","Random order with no age gradient"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In racemose inflorescence the axis keeps growing and produces flowers laterally, so the older flowers lie lower and the younger ones lie towards the apex (acropetal). Basipetal order is the feature of cymose inflorescence.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A leaf whose lamina is deeply incised is always a compound leaf.\nStatement II: A leaf is compound only when the incisions of the lamina reach up to the midrib, breaking it into leaflets.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A lamina can be incised and still be simple, as long as the incisions do not touch the midrib. So depth of incision alone does not make a leaf compound. Only incisions that reach the midrib and break the lamina into leaflets make it compound.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Many pulses of daily use belong to one of the families below (tick the correct answer)","image":"","options":["Solanaceae","Fabaceae","Liliaceae","Poaceae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pulses such as gram, arhar, moong, soybean and pea are legumes. Legumes belong to the family Fabaceae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The growth of a root in length is mainly due to the cells of which region?","image":"","options":["Root cap","Region of elongation","Region of maturation","Root hair zone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cells just proximal to the meristematic zone elongate and enlarge rapidly, and this is what increases root length. The meristem adds cells, but the lengthening itself happens in the region of elongation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student finds a green, leaf-like structure on a twig and must decide whether it is a single leaf or a leaflet of a compound leaf. Which observation would best confirm that it is a leaflet?","image":"","options":["It has a prominent midrib with reticulate venation","No bud is present in its axil, while a bud is present in the axil of the petiole of the common axis bearing it","It is borne at a node of the stem in an alternate arrangement","Its lamina shows incisions that do not reach the midrib"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An axillary bud is found in the axil of the petiole of both simple and compound leaves, but never in the axil of a leaflet. Having a midrib, being borne at a node, or having shallow incisions does not show that a structure is a leaflet. Incisions that do not reach the midrib in fact indicate a simple leaf.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower cannot be divided into two similar halves by any vertical plane passing through its centre. Such a flower is described as asymmetric. Which of the following is an example?","image":"","options":["Datura","Canna","Cassia","Chilli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Canna is the standard example of an asymmetric (irregular) flower. Datura and chilli are actinomorphic. Cassia is zygomorphic, since it can be halved in one vertical plane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) China rose\n(b) Calotropis\n(c) Alstonia\n(d) Neem\nList-II:\n(i) Whorled phyllotaxy\n(ii) Pinnately compound leaf\n(iii) Alternate phyllotaxy\n(iv) Opposite phyllotaxy\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"China rose has alternate phyllotaxy, Calotropis has opposite phyllotaxy and Alstonia has whorled phyllotaxy. Neem bears pinnately compound leaves.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Venation is a term used to describe the pattern of arrangement of","image":"","options":["Floral organs","Flower in inflorescence","Veins and veinlets in a lamina","All of them"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Venation refers specifically to how veins and veinlets are arranged in the leaf blade (lamina). It may be reticulate or parallel.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the majority of dicotyledonous plants, the primary root is formed by the direct elongation of which part of the embryo?","image":"","options":["Plumule","Cotyledon","Radicle","Base of the stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The primary root of most dicots forms when the radicle elongates directly. The plumule gives rise to the stem, not the root.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ovary of mustard appears two-chambered.\nReason (R): In parietal placentation, a one-chambered ovary becomes two-chambered due to the formation of a false septum.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mustard has parietal placentation. Its basically one-chambered ovary becomes two-chambered because a false septum forms, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about placentation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In parietal placentation, the ovary is one-chambered but becomes two-chambered due to a false septum, as in mustard","In free central placentation, ovules are borne on a central axis and septa are absent, as in Dianthus","In basal placentation, the placenta develops at the base of the ovary and bears a single ovule, as in marigold","In marginal placentation, the placenta forms a ridge along the dorsal suture of the ovary, as in pea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In marginal placentation, as in pea, the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture, not the dorsal suture. The ovules are borne on this ridge in two rows. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following flowers are the carpels free from one another (apocarpous gynoecium)?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Tomato","China rose","Lotus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Lotus and rose have free carpels, so their gynoecium is apocarpous. Mustard, tomato and china rose all have fused carpels, so their gynoecium is syncarpous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have non-endospermous (non-endospermic) seeds?\nCastor, bean, gram, pea, maize, orchid, wheat","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bean, gram and pea are non-endospermous dicots, and orchids are an exception among monocots because their seeds lack endosperm, giving four. Castor is an endospermic dicot, and cereals such as maize and wheat have bulky endosperm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show imbricate aestivation?\nCassia, gulmohur, Calotropis, cotton, lady's finger, china rose","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only Cassia and gulmohur are imbricate. Calotropis is valvate, while cotton, lady's finger and china rose are twisted.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a racemose inflorescence, which of the following correctly describes the main axis and the order in which flowers are borne?","image":"","options":["The main axis continues to grow and flowers are borne laterally in acropetal succession","The main axis ends in a flower and flowers are borne in basipetal order","The main axis continues to grow and flowers are borne in basipetal order","The main axis ends in a flower and flowers are borne laterally in acropetal succession"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In racemose inflorescences the main axis keeps growing, so new flowers form near the apex. Older flowers lie towards the base, which is acropetal succession. A terminating axis with basipetal order describes cymose inflorescences.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The potato (Solanum tuberosum) grows underground, yet it is considered a stem and not a root. Which reason best justifies this?","image":"","options":["It stores reserve food material","It grows inside the soil away from light","It bears nodes with buds that can grow into new shoots","It develops directly from the radicle of the embryo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Nodes with buds are a defining stem feature, and they also allow vegetative propagation, so the underground potato is a stem. Food storage is a function shared by roots, so it cannot decide the matter. Growing in soil and arising from the radicle are root characteristics.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Petals of most flowers are brightly coloured.\nReason (R): Bright petals attract insects that bring about pollination.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that petals are usually brightly coloured to attract insects for pollination. The reason therefore directly explains the assertion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In a cymose inflorescence the main axis ends in a flower, so the oldest flower lies at the tip of the axis","When a shoot tip transforms into a flower, the flower is always solitary","The ray florets of sunflower have a superior ovary because the other floral parts arise below the ovary","In a racemose inflorescence the youngest flowers are found nearest the apex of the main axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The ray florets of sunflower are epigynous, so their ovary is inferior and the other parts arise above it. The other three statements are correct: cymose flowers develop basipetally from a terminal flower, a transformed shoot tip is always solitary, and racemose flowers form acropetally.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a floral diagram, a dot is drawn at the top. What does this dot indicate?","image":"","options":["Position of the bract","Position of the mother axis with respect to the flower","Position of the stigma","Number of carpels in the ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The dot at the top of a floral diagram marks the position of the mother axis relative to the flower. The floral whorls are then drawn around the centre, with calyx outermost and gynoecium innermost.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Long, thin, flexible petioles help keep the leaf cool.\nReason (R): Such petioles allow the leaf blade to flutter in the wind, which also brings fresh air to the leaf surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Flexible petioles let the blade flutter in the wind. This fluttering is what cools the leaf and refreshes the air at its surface, so R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Node\n(b) Internode\n(c) Plumule\n(d) Axillary bud\nList-II\n(i) Embryonic part that gives rise to the stem\n(ii) Portion of stem between two leaf-bearing points\n(iii) Develops into a branch\n(iv) Point on stem where a leaf is borne","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A node bears the leaf, and the internode lies between two nodes. The plumule forms the stem, and the axillary bud in the leaf axil grows into a branch. The common trap is swapping node and internode.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following flowers have a superior ovary?\nMustard, China rose, Brinjal, Tomato, Plum, Guava, Peach, Cucumber","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mustard, china rose, brinjal and tomato (Solanaceae, where the ovary is superior) have hypogynous flowers with a superior ovary. Plum and peach are perigynous, with a half-inferior ovary. Guava and cucumber are epigynous, with an inferior ovary.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the floral bud of Calotropis, the petals of the corolla only touch one another at their margins and do not overlap. This type of aestivation is called:","image":"","options":["Twisted","Imbricate","Valvate","Vexillary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When the members of a whorl meet edge to edge without overlapping, the aestivation is valvate, as in Calotropis. Twisted, imbricate and vexillary aestivation all involve some overlapping of margins.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Rose and lotus possess an apocarpous gynoecium.\nStatement II: In free central placentation, the ovules are borne on a central axis and septa are present.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rose and lotus do have free carpels, so Statement I is correct. Free central placentation is defined by the absence of septa, so Statement II is incorrect. A central axis with septa describes axile placentation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an inflorescence where flowers are borne laterally in an acropetal succession, the position of the youngest floral bud in the floral axis shall be","image":"","options":["Proximal","Distal","Intercalary","Any where"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In acropetal succession, older flowers are at the base and younger flowers are towards the apex. The youngest bud is therefore at the distal end of the axis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a pinnately compound leaf such as that of neem, the rachis represents which part of a leaf?","image":"","options":["Petiole","Midrib","Leaf base","Stipule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The leaflets of a pinnately compound leaf are borne on a common axis, the rachis, which represents the midrib of the leaf. It is not the petiole, which lies below the leaflet-bearing region.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the gynoecium is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Each ovary bears one or more ovules attached to a flattened, cushion-like placenta","The style is the enlarged basal part of the carpel on which the ovary lies","Carpels are fused in a syncarpous gynoecium, as in tomato","After fertilisation, the ovules develop into seeds and the ovary matures into a fruit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ovary is the enlarged basal part of the carpel, and the style is the elongated tube above it that connects it to the stigma. The statement reverses the positions of the ovary and the style.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In lily, the stamens are attached to the perianth. Such stamens are described as:","image":"","options":["Epipetalous","Polyadelphous","Epiphyllous","Monoadelphous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Stamens attached to the perianth are called epiphyllous, as in lily. Epipetalous means attached to petals, as in brinjal.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A drupe develops from a monocarpellary superior ovary and is one-seeded.\nStatement II: In coconut, the mesocarp is fleshy and edible, just as in mango.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly describes drupes such as mango and coconut. Statement II is wrong because the coconut mesocarp is fibrous, not fleshy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are main functions of the root system as described in NCERT?\n(i) Absorption of water and minerals\n(ii) Anchorage of plant parts\n(iii) Storage of reserve food material\n(iv) Synthesis of plant growth regulators\n(v) Photosynthesis\n(vi) Bearing flowers and fruits","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Items (i) to (iv) are the four main root functions. Photosynthesis is a leaf function, and bearing flowers and fruits belongs to the stem or shoot system.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Leaves originate from the shoot apical meristem. In what order are they arranged on the stem?","image":"","options":["Basipetal","Acropetal","Random","Centrifugal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Leaves are formed in acropetal order, so the older leaves are lower and the younger ones are nearer the apex. Basipetal order describes flowers in a cymose inflorescence, not leaves.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Solanaceae, the potato (Solanum tuberosum) is an example of a plant whose underground part is a modified root.\nStatement II: In Solanaceae, the gynoecium is bicarpellary with obliquely placed carpels, and the ovary is bilocular with a swollen placenta bearing many ovules.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the potato as an underground stem, not a root, so Statement I is wrong. Statement II correctly gives the bicarpellary, obliquely placed, bilocular gynoecium with swollen axile placenta.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which option gives the correct order of regions in a root tip, starting from the apex and moving upward towards the stem?","image":"","options":["Root cap → region of elongation → region of meristematic activity → region of maturation","Root cap → region of meristematic activity → region of elongation → region of maturation","Region of meristematic activity → root cap → region of maturation → region of elongation","Root cap → region of maturation → region of elongation → region of meristematic activity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The root cap covers the apex, and the meristematic zone lies a few millimetres above it. Elongation happens just proximal to the meristem, and maturation lies proximal to elongation. A common trap is placing elongation before cell division.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Stipules\n(B) Pulvinus\n(C) Midrib\n(D) Petiole\nList-II\n(I) Middle prominent vein of the lamina\n(II) Swollen leaf base in some legumes\n(III) Two small leaf-like lateral structures at the leaf base\n(IV) Helps hold the blade to light\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Stipules are paired leaf-like outgrowths of the leaf base, and the pulvinus is a swollen leaf base. The midrib is the central prominent vein, and the petiole holds the blade towards light.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A stamen that does not produce fertile pollen grains is called a:","image":"","options":["Staminode","Pistillode","Pollinium","Connective"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT calls a sterile stamen a staminode. A pistillode is a sterile carpel, and the other two terms do not mean a sterile stamen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by many roots arising from the base of the stem, forming a fibrous root system.\nStatement II: In the banyan tree, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle and are called adventitious roots.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Monocots such as wheat replace the short-lived primary root with fibrous roots that arise from the stem base. Roots that do not come from the radicle, as in banyan, Monstera and grass, are adventitious. Both statements therefore agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Root cap\nB. Region of meristematic activity\nC. Region of elongation\nD. Region of maturation\nList-II\nI. Rapid enlargement of cells causing increase in length\nII. Differentiation of cells and formation of root hairs\nIII. Protection of the tender root apex\nIV. Repeated division of small cells with dense protoplasm\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The root cap protects the tip, and the meristem divides repeatedly. The elongation zone enlarges cells to lengthen the root, and the maturation zone differentiates cells and bears root hairs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about leaves are correct?\n(i) Veins act as channels for transporting water, minerals and food materials.\n(ii) Stipules are borne at the tip of the lamina.\n(iii) The midrib is the middle prominent vein of the lamina.\n(iv) The bud in the axil of a leaf later develops into a branch.\n(v) Parallel venation is characteristic of most dicotyledons.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (iv) are correct. Stipules are borne at the leaf base, not the lamina tip, and parallel venation is typical of most monocots, not dicots.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the root tip are correct?\n(i) The root cap is present at the apex of the root.\n(ii) The meristematic region lies a few millimetres above the root cap.\n(iii) Cells of the meristematic region have dense protoplasm.\n(iv) Root hairs absorb water and minerals from the soil.\n(v) Root hairs develop from cortical cells.\n(vi) The region of maturation lies distal (closer to the tip) to the region of elongation.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i) to (iv) are correct. Root hairs arise from epidermal cells, not cortical cells, so (v) is wrong. The maturation zone is proximal to the elongation zone, not closer to the tip, so (vi) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (use) with List-II (Solanaceae plant):\nList-I\n(A) Spice\n(B) Medicine\n(C) Ornamental\n(D) Food\nList-II\n(I) Petunia\n(II) Brinjal\n(III) Chilli\n(IV) Ashwagandha","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct pairs are chilli as a spice, ashwagandha as a medicine, petunia as an ornamental and brinjal as a food. Do not confuse chilli (spice) with the medicinal plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Hypogynous flower\nB. Perigynous flower\nC. Epigynous flower\nD. Zygomorphic flower\nList-II\ni. Pea\nii. Cucumber\niii. Plum\niv. Mustard\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct pairs are:\n- Mustard: hypogynous, with a superior ovary.\n- Plum: perigynous, with a half-inferior ovary.\n- Cucumber: epigynous, with an inferior ovary.\n- Pea: zygomorphic.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Position of floral parts)\n(A) Hypogynous\n(B) Perigynous\n(C) Epigynous\nList-II (Example)\n(I) Cucumber\n(II) Brinjal\n(III) Peach\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I","A-III, B-II, C-I","A-II, B-I, C-III","A-I, B-III, C-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Brinjal is hypogynous with a superior ovary, peach is perigynous with a half-inferior ovary, and cucumber is epigynous with an inferior ovary.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the papilionaceous corolla of a pea flower, the largest petal is called the","image":"","options":["Keel","Wing","Standard","Sepal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The largest petal in vexillary aestivation is the standard (vexillum). The two wings are lateral, and the two smallest anterior petals form the keel.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A region of the root tip has very small, thin-walled cells with dense protoplasm that divide repeatedly. This region is the:","image":"","options":["Region of maturation","Region of elongation","Region of meristematic activity","Root cap"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Small size, thin walls, dense protoplasm and repeated division are the features of the meristematic region. This region lies a few millimetres above the root cap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Castor seeds are described as endospermic seeds.\nReason (R): In castor, the endosperm formed as a result of double fertilisation persists in the mature seed as a food-storing tissue.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A seed is called endospermic because its endosperm stays in the mature seed and stores food, which is true of castor. R therefore explains A. In seeds such as gram and pea, the endosperm is absent in the mature seed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the root hairs of a seedling are damaged during transplanting, its uptake of water and minerals falls, even though its root cap and meristematic region are intact.\nStatement II: Root hairs are produced by cells of the region of elongation, which then stop dividing.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Root hairs are the structures that absorb water and minerals, so losing them reduces uptake even if the tip is unharmed. Statement II is wrong because root hairs arise from epidermal cells in the region of maturation, not the region of elongation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the maize grain, the single large, shield-shaped cotyledon is called the scutellum.\nStatement II: In the maize embryo, the plumule is enclosed in a sheath called the coleorhiza.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The scutellum is indeed the shield-shaped cotyledon of maize. However, the plumule is sheathed by the coleoptile, while the coleorhiza encloses the radicle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A leaf is a lateral outgrowth of the stem that develops exogenously at a node.\nStatement II: On a shoot, the youngest leaves are found towards the base and the oldest near the apex.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Leaves arise exogenously at nodes, so Statement I is correct. Because leaves form in acropetal order, the youngest leaves are near the apex, not the base, so Statement II reverses the actual arrangement.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower has the following features:\n(i) Its floral appendages are in multiples of 4.\n(ii) It can be cut into two equal halves along any radial plane passing through the centre.\n(iii) The gynoecium occupies the highest position on the thalamus, and the other parts lie below it.\nWhich option correctly describes this flower?","image":"","options":["Tetramerous, zygomorphic, hypogynous with inferior ovary","Tetramerous, actinomorphic, hypogynous with superior ovary","Pentamerous, actinomorphic, epigynous with inferior ovary","Tetramerous, actinomorphic, perigynous with half-inferior ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Parts in multiples of 4 make the flower tetramerous, and division along any radial plane means it is actinomorphic. A gynoecium in the highest position marks a hypogynous flower, whose ovary is superior, as in mustard. The trap is linking 'highest position' with an inferior ovary.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines transverse sections of two ovaries.\nOvary X: several chambers separated by septa, with ovules attached to a placenta in the central axis.\nOvary Y: a single chamber with no septa, with ovules borne on a central column.\nWhich pair of plants could X and Y belong to, respectively?","image":"","options":["Argemone and Dianthus","Lemon and Marigold","Lemon and Primrose","Pea and Primrose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"X is a multilocular ovary with an axial placenta, which is axile placentation as in lemon. Y has a central axis with no septa, which is free central placentation as in Primrose. Argemone is parietal, marigold is basal and pea is marginal, so those pairings fail.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Plants of the family Solanaceae typically produce which type of fruit?","image":"","options":["Drupe or nut","Legume or pod","Berry or capsule","Siliqua or achene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the fruits of Solanaceae as berry (e.g., tomato) or capsule. Drupes such as mango and coconut belong to other families.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a floral bud shows the following: there are five petals; the largest posterior petal overlaps two lateral petals, and these two lateral petals in turn overlap the two smallest anterior petals. Which of the following correctly identifies this arrangement?","image":"","options":["Imbricate aestivation; the largest petal is the keel and the smallest pair forms the wings; seen in Cassia","Vexillary aestivation; the largest petal is the standard, the lateral pair are wings and the anterior pair form the keel; seen in pea","Twisted aestivation; the largest petal is the standard and the anterior pair are wings; seen in cotton","Vexillary aestivation; the largest petal is the standard, the lateral pair form the keel and the anterior pair are wings; seen in gulmohur"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"This is vexillary (papilionaceous) aestivation of pea and bean. In it, the posterior standard overlaps the lateral wings, which overlap the anterior keel. Swapping wings with keel, or choosing Cassia or gulmohur (both imbricate), are the traps.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about seeds:\n(a) Castor is a dicot whose mature seed retains endosperm.\n(b) Orchids are an exception among monocots as their seeds are non-endospermic.\n(c) In a maize seed, the coleorhiza is the sheath enclosing the plumule.\n(d) The hilum is the scar of attachment of the seed to the fruit, and the micropyle lies above it.\n(e) In gram seeds, reserve food is stored mainly in the endosperm.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(a), (b) and (d) only","(a), (c) and (d) only","(b), (d) and (e) only","(a), (b), (c) and (e) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The coleorhiza sheathes the radicle, while the coleoptile encloses the plumule, so (c) is wrong. Gram is non-endospermous and stores food in its fleshy cotyledons, so (e) is wrong. Only (a), (b) and (d) are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The stamens of lily are described as epiphyllous rather than epipetalous.\nReason (R): In lily, the calyx and corolla are not distinct from each other and together are termed perianth.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lily has no separate petals, only perianth members, so its stamens are attached to the perianth and are called epiphyllous. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following have flowers with an inferior ovary?\nGuava, cucumber, ray florets of sunflower, plum, mustard, brinjal, peach","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Guava, cucumber and the ray florets of sunflower are epigynous, so their ovary is inferior. Plum and peach are perigynous with a half-inferior ovary, which is the key trap. Mustard and brinjal are hypogynous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower has the following features:\n- Its floral appendages occur in multiples of four.\n- It can be cut into two equal halves along any radial plane through its centre.\n- Its gynoecium occupies the highest position on the thalamus, with all other parts below it.\nWhich description fits this flower?","image":"","options":["Trimerous, zygomorphic, epigynous with inferior ovary","Tetramerous, zygomorphic, perigynous with half-inferior ovary","Tetramerous, actinomorphic, hypogynous with superior ovary","Pentamerous, actinomorphic, hypogynous with superior ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Appendages in multiples of 4 make the flower tetramerous. Equal halves along any radial plane make it actinomorphic. A gynoecium at the highest position means hypogynous with a superior ovary, as in mustard.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a compound leaf, an axillary bud is present in the axil of each leaflet.\nStatement II: Leaves originate from the shoot apical meristem and are arranged in acropetal order.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In a compound leaf, a bud is present only in the axil of the petiole, not in the axils of the leaflets, so Statement I is wrong. This feature also helps tell a compound leaf apart from a branch bearing simple leaves. Statement II correctly describes how leaves originate and how they are arranged.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The placenta is attached to the developing seed near the","image":"","options":["Testa","Hilum","Micropyle","Chalaza"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The hilum is the scar on the seed coat marking where the developing seed was attached to the placenta (via the funicle).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following and choose the correct option\nColumn I — Column II\nA. Aleurone layer — i. without fertilization\nB. Parthenocarpic fruit — ii. Nutrition\nC. Ovule — iii. Double fertilization\nD. Endosperm — iv. Seed","image":"","options":["A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The aleurone layer is a protein-rich layer associated with nutrition, and parthenocarpic fruits develop without fertilization. The ovule develops into the seed, and the endosperm results from double fertilization.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A leaflet of a compound leaf can be distinguished from a simple leaf by checking for a bud in its axil.\nReason (R): An axillary bud is present in the axil of the petiole of a leaf but is absent in the axil of the leaflets of a compound leaf.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A simple leaf has a bud in the axil of its petiole, whereas a leaflet has no axillary bud. R therefore states exactly why the presence or absence of an axillary bud separates the two.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of aestivation) with List-II (example).\nList-I:\n(a) Valvate\n(b) Twisted\n(c) Imbricate\n(d) Vexillary\nList-II:\n(i) Gulmohur\n(ii) Bean\n(iii) Calotropis\n(iv) Cotton","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Calotropis is valvate, cotton is twisted, gulmohur is imbricate and bean is vexillary. The common trap is mixing up gulmohur (imbricate) with cotton (twisted).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have zygomorphic flowers?\nPea, datura, gulmohur, chilli, bean, Cassia, mustard, canna","image":"","options":["3","5","6","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Pea, gulmohur, bean and Cassia are zygomorphic, which makes four. Datura, chilli and mustard are actinomorphic. Canna is asymmetric, not zygomorphic, which is the usual trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a cymose inflorescence, the main axis terminates in a flower and is therefore limited in growth.\nStatement II: In a cymose inflorescence, the flowers are borne in acropetal succession.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a cyme the axis ends in a flower, so its growth is limited. Because the oldest flower is at the tip, the flowers develop in basipetal order, and acropetal order is the feature of racemose inflorescence.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following flowers are the calyx and corolla not distinguishable from each other, so that they are together termed the perianth?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Pea","Lily","China rose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT cites lily as the example in which calyx and corolla are not distinct and are called the perianth. Mustard, pea and china rose all have a separate calyx and corolla.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Sepals are generally green, leaf-like and protect the flower in the bud stage","Corolla may be tubular, bell-shaped, funnel-shaped or wheel-shaped","In imbricate aestivation, the margins of sepals or petals overlap in one particular direction throughout the whorl","In valvate aestivation, sepals or petals just touch one another at the margins without overlapping"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In imbricate aestivation the margins overlap but not in any particular direction. A regular one-directional overlap describes twisted aestivation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower cannot be divided into two similar halves by any vertical plane passing through its centre. Such a flower is found in:","image":"","options":["Datura","Canna","Cassia","Chilli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A flower that no vertical plane can divide into similar halves is asymmetric (irregular), as in canna. Datura and chilli are actinomorphic, and Cassia is zygomorphic.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show axile placentation?\nChina rose, Tomato, Lemon, Mustard, Pea, Dianthus, Argemone","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only china rose, tomato and lemon have axile placentation. Mustard and Argemone are parietal, pea is marginal and Dianthus is free central.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A bud is present in the axil of the petiole of both simple and compound leaves, and such buds are borne on the stem at nodes.\nStatement II: Checking for the presence of a bud in its axil can help decide whether a leaf-like structure is an individual leaflet or an entire compound leaf.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Axillary buds occur in the axil of the petiole of simple and compound leaves at the nodes of the stem, so Statement I is correct. They are absent in the axils of leaflets, which makes the bud a reliable test for a whole leaf versus a leaflet, so Statement II is also correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A hypothetical flower is described by the floral formula Br % ⚥ $P_{(3+3)}\\,A_{3+3}\\,\\overline{G}_{(3)}$. Which description correctly matches this formula?","image":"","options":["Ebracteate, actinomorphic, bisexual; perianth free; six stamens fused; ovary superior, tricarpellary, syncarpous","Bracteate, zygomorphic, unisexual; perianth fused; six stamens free; ovary inferior, tricarpellary, apocarpous","Bracteate, zygomorphic, bisexual; perianth of six fused segments in two whorls; six free stamens in two whorls; ovary inferior, tricarpellary, syncarpous","Bracteate, actinomorphic, bisexual; perianth fused; six stamens epiphyllous; ovary superior, tricarpellary, syncarpous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Br means bracteate, % means zygomorphic and ⚥ means bisexual. The bracket around 3+3 shows a fused perianth, while unbracketed A3+3 shows free stamens. A line above G means an inferior ovary, and (3) means three fused carpels.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Tap root system\n(b) Fibrous root system\n(c) Adventitious roots\n(d) Secondary roots\nList-II\n(i) Many roots arising from the stem base after the primary root dies early\n(ii) First-order laterals borne on the primary root\n(iii) Primary root together with all its branches\n(iv) Roots arising from plant parts other than the radicle\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The pairings are as follows: the tap root system is the primary root with its branches; the fibrous system arises from the stem base; adventitious roots come from non-radicle parts; secondary roots are the first-order laterals of the primary root.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which type of placentation does the placenta form a ridge along the ventral suture of the ovary, with the ovules borne on it in two rows?","image":"","options":["Axile","Parietal","Marginal","Basal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In marginal placentation, as in pea, the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture and carries ovules in two rows. Parietal placentation also bears ovules on the ovary wall, but it has no single ventral ridge.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the bud of a bean flower, which petals are overlapped by another petal and also themselves overlap other petals?","image":"","options":["The standard","The two wings","The two keel petals","Both the standard and the keel petals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The standard overlaps the wings, and the wings overlap the keel. So only the wings are both overlapped and overlapping. The standard is never overlapped, and the keel petals overlap nothing.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the stem as described for flowering plants?","image":"","options":["Conduction of water, minerals and photosynthates","Spreading out branches bearing leaves, flowers and fruits","Absorption of water and minerals from the soil through fine hair-like outgrowths","Vegetative propagation in some plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Absorbing water and minerals from the soil through root hairs is a function of the root system. The stem conducts water and minerals but does not absorb them from the soil. Spreading out branches and, in some plants, vegetative propagation are stem functions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which pair of plants does the gynoecium have more than one carpel, with the carpels remaining free from each other?","image":"","options":["Mustard and tomato","Lotus and rose","China rose and lemon","Pea and bean"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Free carpels make the gynoecium apocarpous, as in lotus and rose. Mustard, tomato, china rose and lemon have fused (syncarpous) carpels, and pea has a single carpel.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a perigynous flower, the gynoecium is in the centre and the other floral parts are located on the rim of the thalamus almost at the same level, so the ovary is half inferior.\nStatement II: In an epigynous flower, the margin of the thalamus grows upward and fuses with the ovary, and the other floral parts arise below the ovary.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly describes perigyny. In epigyny the thalamus encloses and fuses with the ovary, but the other floral parts arise above the ovary, not below it. That is why the ovary is called inferior, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following plants bears flowers that can be divided into two similar halves in only one particular vertical plane?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Datura","Pea","Chilli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Division into similar halves along only one vertical plane defines a zygomorphic (bilaterally symmetrical) flower, and pea is an example. Mustard, datura and chilli have actinomorphic flowers, which can be halved along any radial plane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plants is most likely to have leaves with parallel venation?","image":"","options":["Mustard","China rose","Guava","Wheat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Parallel venation is characteristic of most monocots, and wheat is a monocot. Mustard, china rose and guava are dicots, which generally have reticulate venation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student lists the following features for Solanum nigrum (family Solanaceae):\nI. Calyx of five united, persistent sepals with valvate aestivation\nII. Five stamens attached to the petals\nIII. Bicarpellary syncarpous ovary that is inferior with parietal placentation\nIV. Leaves alternate, simple, exstipulate with reticulate venation\nWhich of these features are correct?","image":"","options":["I, II and III only","I, II and IV only","II, III and IV only","I and IV only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Solanaceae has a persistent valvate gamosepalous calyx, epipetalous stamens, and alternate, simple, exstipulate leaves with reticulate venation. So I, II and IV are correct. Statement III is wrong because the ovary is superior and bilocular, with a swollen axile placenta.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A flower is regarded as a modified shoot.\nReason (R): During flower formation, the shoot apical meristem changes to a floral meristem, the internodes do not elongate, and floral appendages are produced laterally at successive nodes instead of leaves.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The shoot nature of the flower is shown by its nodes, which bear floral appendages in place of leaves on a condensed axis derived from the shoot apex. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a germinating seed, the stem of a flowering plant develops from which part of the embryo?","image":"","options":["Radicle","Plumule","Cotyledon","Hilum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The stem is the ascending axis and develops from the plumule of the embryo. The radicle gives rise to the primary root. Cotyledons and the hilum do not form the axis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In most monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by many roots that arise from the base of the stem.\nStatement II: In most dicotyledonous plants, the radicle elongates directly to form a primary root, which bears secondary and tertiary roots.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I describes the fibrous root system, as in wheat. Statement II describes the tap root system, as in mustard. Both statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An axillary bud is present in the axil of the petiole of both simple and compound leaves.\nStatement II: Each leaflet of a compound leaf bears an axillary bud in its axil.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A bud occurs in the axil of the petiole in both simple and compound leaves. However, no bud occurs in the axil of the individual leaflets of a compound leaf, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A gardener places a cut piece of grass stem in moist soil, and roots soon develop from its nodes. These roots are best described as:","image":"","options":["Secondary roots, because they are branches of the primary root","Tap roots, because they grow downward into the soil","Tertiary roots, because they arise late in development","Adventitious roots, because they arise from a part other than the radicle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"These roots come from stem nodes, not from the radicle or its branches, so they are adventitious; NCERT gives grass as an example. Secondary and tertiary roots are branches of a radicle-derived primary root.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plants is used to extract the blue dye?","image":"","options":["Trifolium","Indigofera","Lupin","Cassia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Indigofera, a member of Fabaceae, is the source of the blue dye indigo.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of aestivation) with List-II (example).\nList-I:\nA. Valvate\nB. Twisted\nC. Imbricate\nD. Vexillary\nList-II:\nI. Cassia\nII. Calotropis\nIII. Bean\nIV. Lady's finger","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct matches are:\n- Valvate: Calotropis, where the margins only touch.\n- Twisted: lady's finger, where each margin overlaps the next in one direction.\n- Imbricate: Cassia, where margins overlap in no fixed direction.\n- Vexillary: bean.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In monocotyledons, the leaf base commonly expands into a structure that partially or wholly covers the stem. This structure is the:","image":"","options":["Pulvinus","Petiole","Sheath","Midrib"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In monocots the leaf base expands into a sheath that wraps the stem partially or completely. The pulvinus is a swollen leaf base found in some legumes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which plant has whorled phyllotaxy, where more than two leaves arise at a single node?","image":"","options":["China rose","Guava","Alstonia","Mustard"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In Alstonia more than two leaves arise at each node and form a whorl. China rose and mustard have alternate phyllotaxy, and guava has opposite phyllotaxy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Coconut is classified as a drupe even though its mesocarp is fibrous rather than fleshy.\nReason (R): Drupes are one-seeded fruits that develop from monocarpellary superior ovaries.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A drupe is defined by its origin from a monocarpellary superior ovary and by being one-seeded. It is not defined by whether the mesocarp is fleshy, as in mango, or fibrous, as in coconut. R therefore explains why coconut is a drupe.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which member of the family Solanaceae is cited in NCERT as a source of a fumigatory?","image":"","options":["Belladonna","Petunia","Ashwagandha","Tobacco"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Tobacco is the fumigatory member of Solanaceae. Belladonna and ashwagandha are medicinal plants, and petunia is an ornamental.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show alternate phyllotaxy?\nChina rose, Mustard, Guava, Sunflower, Calotropis, Alstonia","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"China rose, mustard and sunflower bear a single leaf at each node, giving three plants with alternate phyllotaxy. Guava and Calotropis are opposite, and Alstonia is whorled.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure in a maize seed) with List-II (description) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(A) Scutellum\n(B) Aleurone layer\n(C) Coleoptile\n(D) Coleorhiza\nList-II:\n(i) Sheath enclosing the radicle\n(ii) Large, shield-shaped single cotyledon\n(iii) Sheath enclosing the plumule\n(iv) Proteinous layer separating the endosperm from the embryo","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The scutellum is the shield-shaped cotyledon, and the aleurone layer is the proteinous outer layer of the endosperm. The coleoptile sheathes the plumule, while the coleorhiza sheathes the radicle. Swapping these two sheaths is the usual trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of a flower and the position of its ovary is incorrectly matched?","image":"","options":["Mustard – superior ovary","Plum – half-inferior ovary","Cucumber – inferior ovary","Brinjal – inferior ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Brinjal has a hypogynous flower, so its ovary is superior. Its inferior-ovary label is the mismatch. Plum is perigynous (half-inferior) and cucumber is epigynous (inferior).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An axillary bud is present in the axil of each leaflet of a pinnately compound leaf.\nReason (R): In a pinnately compound leaf, the leaflets are borne on a common axis, the rachis, which represents the midrib of the leaf.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A bud is found only in the axil of the petiole of a compound leaf, never in the axil of its leaflets, so A is false. R correctly describes the rachis, as seen in neem.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Coconut is classified as a drupe.\nReason (R): The mesocarp of the coconut fruit is fibrous.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Coconut is a drupe because, like mango, it develops from a monocarpellary superior ovary and is one-seeded. Its fibrous mesocarp is a true fact, but it is not what makes a fruit a drupe; mango is also a drupe and has a fleshy mesocarp.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plants is cited by NCERT as an example of a fibrous root system?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Wheat","Pea","Gram"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Wheat is a monocot. Its short-lived primary root is replaced by many roots arising from the stem base, which form a fibrous root system. Mustard has a tap root system, and pea and gram are dicots.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The floral formula of mustard is written as ⊕ K2+2 C4 A2+4 G(2), with the ovary shown as superior. Which of the following interpretations of this formula and its floral diagram is correct?","image":"","options":["The bracket in G(2) shows adhesion of the two carpels with the stamens","The androecium has six stamens in two whorls, and the ovary is superior","The dot drawn at the top of the floral diagram represents the bract","The corolla consists of four united (gamopetalous) petals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A2+4 indicates six stamens in two whorls, and mustard is hypogynous with a superior ovary. A bracket indicates fusion (cohesion) within a whorl, whereas adhesion is shown by a line above the symbols. The dot marks the mother axis, and C4 without a bracket means the four petals are free.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ovary of mustard shows parietal placentation, yet it appears two-chambered.\nReason (R): A false septum forms in the originally one-chambered ovary of mustard.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In parietal placentation the ovules arise on the inner wall of a one-chambered ovary. In mustard and Argemone, a false septum divides this ovary into two chambers, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In both lemon and Primrose the ovules are borne on a central axis, but only in lemon is the ovary divided into chambers.\nStatement II: In basal placentation, as in marigold, many ovules are attached to a placenta at the base of the ovary.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Lemon has axile placentation in a multilocular ovary, while Primrose has free central placentation with no septa, so Statement I is correct. In basal placentation only a single ovule is attached at the base, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the floral formula of a Solanaceae flower, a line is drawn above the symbols C and A.\nReason (R): Adhesion between floral whorls is shown by a line drawn above the symbols, and in Solanaceae the stamens are attached to the petals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Solanaceae stamens are epipetalous, which is adhesion between the corolla and androecium. Adhesion is shown by a line above the symbols, so R correctly explains A. Brackets would show fusion within a whorl, not adhesion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the pericarp of a mango do we eat?","image":"","options":["Epicarp","Mesocarp","Endocarp","Seed coat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In mango, the middle mesocarp is fleshy and edible. The epicarp is a thin outer skin and the endocarp is the hard stony layer around the seed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A pair of leaves arises at each node of a plant, and the two leaves lie opposite each other. Which of the following plants shows this arrangement?","image":"","options":["Sunflower","Guava","China rose","Alstonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Guava, like Calotropis, has opposite phyllotaxy. Sunflower and china rose bear a single leaf per node (alternate), while Alstonia bears more than two leaves per node (whorled).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the calyx and corolla of a member of Solanaceae such as Solanum nigrum?","image":"","options":["Calyx polysepalous and twisted; corolla polypetalous and imbricate","Calyx gamosepalous, persistent and valvate; corolla gamopetalous and valvate","Calyx gamosepalous and imbricate; corolla gamopetalous and twisted","Calyx polysepalous and valvate; corolla gamopetalous and vexillary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In Solanaceae, both sepals and petals are five and united. The calyx is persistent, and both whorls show valvate aestivation. Twisted (china rose) and vexillary (pea) corollas belong to other plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of placentation) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Axile\nB. Parietal\nC. Free central\nD. Basal\nList-II\nI. Argemone\nII. Marigold\nIII. Lemon\nIV. Primrose","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lemon has axile placentation, Argemone has parietal, Primrose has free central and marigold has basal placentation. The usual trap is mixing up free central (Primrose) with axile (lemon), since both have ovules on a central axis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In members of Solanaceae, the stamens are epipetalous and the ovary is superior and bilocular with axile placentation.\nStatement II: Coconut, like mango, is a drupe in which the mesocarp is fleshy and edible.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly describes the Solanaceae androecium and gynoecium. Coconut is indeed a drupe, but its mesocarp is fibrous. The fleshy, edible mesocarp belongs to mango, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A reduced leaf is present at the base of the pedicel of a flower. Such a flower is termed:","image":"","options":["Ebracteate","Perigynous","Bracteate","Unisexual"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A bract is a reduced leaf at the base of the pedicel, and flowers that bear one are called bracteate. Ebracteate flowers lack bracts. Perigynous and unisexual refer to ovary position and sex, not to bracts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show twisted aestivation in their corolla?\nChina rose, Cassia, Cotton, Calotropis, Lady's finger, Gulmohur, Pea","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"China rose, cotton and lady's finger show twisted aestivation, where each margin overlaps the next in one direction. Cassia and gulmohur are imbricate, Calotropis is valvate and pea is vexillary.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Aleurone layer\n(b) Scutellum\n(c) Coleoptile\n(d) Tegmen\nList-II\n(i) Sheath enclosing the plumule\n(ii) Inner layer of the seed coat\n(iii) Proteinous layer separating the endosperm from the embryo\n(iv) Large, shield-shaped single cotyledon\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The aleurone layer is the proteinous layer around the endosperm, the scutellum is the shield-shaped cotyledon of maize, the coleoptile sheathes the plumule, and the tegmen is the inner seed coat layer. The outer seed coat layer is the testa.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In members of Solanaceae, the calyx is gamosepalous and shows valvate aestivation.\nStatement II: Aestivation is the arrangement of sepals or petals in a floral bud with respect to the members of the other floral whorls.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Solanaceae has five united sepals with valvate aestivation, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because aestivation describes the arrangement of sepals or petals relative to other members of the same whorl, not other whorls.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Roots that arise from any part of the plant other than the radicle are called:","image":"","options":["Tap roots","Adventitious roots","Secondary roots","Tertiary roots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Adventitious roots develop from parts other than the radicle, as in grass, Monstera and banyan. Secondary and tertiary roots are branches of the primary root, which itself comes from the radicle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Salvia and mustard, the filaments of the stamens within the same flower vary in length.\nStatement II: In brinjal, the stamens are united into more than two bundles.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives Salvia and mustard as examples of variation in filament length. Brinjal is the example of epipetalous stamens, while stamens in more than two bundles (polyadelphous) are seen in citrus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the main functions of the root system?","image":"","options":["Absorption of water and minerals from the soil","Providing anchorage to the plant parts","Synthesis of plant growth regulators","Photosynthesis to produce food for the plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The main root functions are absorption, anchorage, storage of reserve food and synthesis of plant growth regulators. Photosynthesis is chiefly a function of leaves, not roots.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have stamens united among themselves into one or more bundles?\nChina rose, pea, citrus, brinjal, lily, mustard","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"China rose (one bundle), pea (two bundles) and citrus (many bundles) show stamens fused among themselves. Brinjal and lily show stamens attached to other floral parts (petals and perianth), and mustard stamens are free but differ in filament length.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the maize grain is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The seed coat is membranous and generally fused with the fruit wall.","The embryo lies in a groove at one end of the bulky endosperm.","The embryo has two cotyledons, one of which is enlarged into the scutellum.","A proteinous aleurone layer forms the outer covering of the endosperm and separates it from the embryo."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Maize is a monocot, so its embryo has only one cotyledon, the large shield-shaped scutellum. All the other statements correctly describe the maize grain.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student marks the terminal 1 cm of a young root with ink dots at equal intervals of 1 mm, starting from the tip. After 24 hours of growth, the dots in one particular part of the root have moved farthest apart. These widely spaced dots most likely lie in:","image":"","options":["The root cap, because it is pushed through the soil","The region of maturation, because root hairs form there","The region of elongation, just behind the meristematic zone","The region of meristematic activity, because its cells divide repeatedly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rapid lengthening and enlargement of cells happens in the region of elongation, so marks there separate the most. Meristematic cells divide but stay small. Cells in the maturation zone have already stopped elongating and are differentiating.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A twig has exactly two leaf-like structures at each node, lying opposite each other. Each structure has an axillary bud only at the base of its stalk. Its several blades are arranged along a common central axis, and no buds occur at the bases of these blades. Which description is correct?","image":"","options":["Simple leaves in whorled phyllotaxy","Palmately compound leaves in alternate phyllotaxy","Pinnately compound leaves in opposite phyllotaxy","Each blade is a simple leaf, and the central axis is a branch"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The blades lack axillary buds, so they are leaflets of one compound leaf, and their attachment along a common axis (rachis) makes the leaf pinnately compound. Two such leaves per node, opposite each other, means opposite phyllotaxy. The axis cannot be a branch, because the blades on it bear no axillary buds.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Stamens are united into a single bundle in china rose but into more than two bundles in citrus.\nStatement II: Stamens attached to the petals, as in brinjal, are described as epiphyllous.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"China rose is monoadelphous and citrus is polyadelphous, so Statement I is correct. Stamens attached to petals, as in brinjal, are epipetalous. The term epiphyllous refers to stamens attached to the perianth, as in lily, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the gynoecium is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In Solanaceae, the ovary is superior and bilocular, with a swollen placenta bearing many ovules in axile placentation","Mustard has a syncarpous gynoecium with parietal placentation","In sunflower, the placenta develops at the base of the ovary and bears many ovules","In pea, the ovules form two rows on a ridge along the ventral suture"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sunflower has basal placentation, in which only a single ovule is attached to the basal placenta, so 'many ovules' is wrong. The other statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Roots developed from parts of the plant other than radicle are called","image":"","options":["Taproots","Fibrous roots","Adventitious roots","Nodular roots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Roots that arise from any part other than the radicle, such as stems or leaves, are termed adventitious roots. Examples include those of grasses, Monstera and the banyan tree.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which type of placentation are the ovules borne in two rows on a ridge along the ventral suture of the ovary, as seen in pea?","image":"","options":["Axile","Marginal","Parietal","Free central"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In marginal placentation the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture and bears ovules in two rows. Pea is the standard example.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The floral formula of mustard (Brassicaceae) is written as $\\oplus$ $K_{2+2}$ $C_4$ $A_{2+4}$ $\\underline{G}_{(2)}$. Which inference drawn from this formula is correct?","image":"","options":["The four sepals are fused, arranged in two whorls of two each","There are six free stamens arranged in two whorls, an outer of two and an inner of four","The gynoecium consists of two free carpels with a superior ovary","The flower is zygomorphic with four petals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a floral formula, fusion is shown by brackets. Since $A_{2+4}$ has no brackets, it means six free stamens in two whorls of 2 and 4. Similarly, $K_{2+2}$ indicates four free sepals. The bracketed $G_{(2)}$ shows two fused carpels (syncarpous), and $\\oplus$ shows an actinomorphic flower.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In silk cotton, all the leaflets of a leaf are attached at a common point at the tip of the petiole. Such a leaf is described as:","image":"","options":["Pinnately compound","Simple with deep incisions","Palmately compound","Simple with reticulate venation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When leaflets arise from a common point at the tip of the petiole, the leaf is palmately compound, as in silk cotton. A pinnately compound leaf has its leaflets arranged along a rachis instead.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a pinnately compound leaf such as that of neem, the leaflets are borne on a common axis. This axis, called the rachis, represents which part of the leaf?","image":"","options":["The petiole","The midrib","The leaf base","The axillary bud"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a pinnately compound leaf the leaflets sit on the rachis, which represents the midrib of the leaf. The petiole lies below the rachis; it is not the axis that bears the leaflets.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Leaves on a stem are arranged in an acropetal order.\nReason (R): Leaves originate from the shoot apical meristem.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Leaves are produced by the shoot apical meristem. So the youngest leaves lie nearest the apex and the older ones lower down, which is acropetal order. R therefore explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The floral formula of mustard is $\\oplus$ ⚥ $K_{2+2}\\,C_4\\,A_{2+4}\\,\\underline{G}_{(2)}$. How many of the following inferences drawn from it are correct?\n(i) The flower is actinomorphic\n(ii) Sepals are arranged in two whorls of two each\n(iii) There are six stamens in two whorls\n(iv) The two carpels are fused\n(v) The petals are united\n(vi) The ovary is inferior","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Inferences (i) to (iv) are correct. C4 has no bracket, so the petals are free, and the underlined G shows a superior ovary, so (v) and (vi) are wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants bear actinomorphic flowers?\nMustard, Datura, Pea, Chilli, Cassia, Canna, Gulmohur","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mustard, Datura and chilli have actinomorphic (radially symmetrical) flowers. Pea, Cassia and gulmohur have zygomorphic flowers. Canna has asymmetric flowers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a mango fruit, the part that is commonly eaten is the:","image":"","options":["Thin outer epicarp","Stony hard endocarp","Fleshy mesocarp","Seed coat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The mango pericarp has three layers: a thin outer epicarp, a fleshy edible middle mesocarp and a stony inner endocarp. The edible part is the mesocarp. Do not confuse this with coconut, where the mesocarp is fibrous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In some leguminous plants the leaf base becomes swollen. What is this swollen leaf base called?","image":"","options":["Stipule","Pulvinus","Leaf sheath","Rachis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A swollen leaf base in some legumes is called the pulvinus. Stipules are small leaf-like outgrowths at the leaf base, a sheath is the expanded leaf base of monocots, and the rachis is the axis of a pinnately compound leaf.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The region of elongation lies proximal to the region of meristematic activity in a root tip.\nReason (R): Cells of the region of meristematic activity are large, with thick walls and sparse protoplasm.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The elongation zone does lie just proximal to the meristematic zone, so A is true. R is false because meristematic cells are very small and thin-walled, with dense protoplasm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"}]});
